Per-CPU Data

Per-core data structures for SMP support. Each core has
its own scheduler state (current thread, run queues, idle
flag) accessed via core ID. On single-core systems, only
core 0's entry is used — no overhead.

The core ID is read from the IPI device's SELF_ID register
(memory-mapped, read-only). Each core's IPI device is at
IPI_BASE + coreId * 16, with SELF_ID at offset 12.

    module oskit.arch

    import oskit.config.{MAX_CORES, NUM_PRIORITIES}

IPI device addresses for reading core ID.

    val IPI_BASE_ADDR = 0x800300
    val IPI_SELF_ID_OFFSET = 12

Read the current core's ID from hardware.

    core_id() -> int
        val addr = IPI_BASE_ADDR + IPI_SELF_ID_OFFSET
        *(*int(addr))

Per-core data. Each core has its own scheduler state
so cores can schedule independently without contention.

    struct CoreLocal
        current_thread: int
        idle: int
        ready_mask: int
        ticks: int
        queue_head: [4]int
        queue_tail: [4]int

    var cores: [8]CoreLocal       // 8 = MAX_CORES

Initialize per-core data for all cores. Called once
during kernel boot before any scheduling begins.

    percpu_init()
        var i = 0
        while i < MAX_CORES
            cores[i].current_thread = -1
            cores[i].idle = 0
            cores[i].ready_mask = 0
            cores[i].ticks = 0
            var p = 0
            while p < NUM_PRIORITIES
                cores[i].queue_head[p] = -1
                cores[i].queue_tail[p] = -1
                p += 1
            i += 1

Get the CoreLocal struct for the current core.

    this_core() -> *CoreLocal
        &cores[core_id()]
